        "updated_assessment": "The defense budget structural assessment is unchanged. Two minor developments this week: (1) PLA air activity was notably reduced during the Trump-Xi summit window \u2014 MND reported only 2 PLAAF sorties on May 13 and zero aircraft on May 16, against the prior baseline of 10-12 sorties daily. Per \u00a719 Watch Indicator 3 and \u00a720 pipeline notes, this is diplomatic sequencing by Beijing, not de-escalation; the analytical task is to track the post-summit baseline that resumes. (2) Trump's 'decision soon' statement on arms sales creates a new explicit near-term delivery watch \u2014 the unresolved HIMARS payment deadline (end of May 2026) now intersects with the US president having publicly placed arms sales inside bilateral China negotiations. The Taipei Times editorial (confirmed by multiple sources) reiterates drone export figures \u2014 136,000+ units worth $115.85M in Q1 2026 \u2014 as confirmation of the defense-industrial diversification trajectory identified last week. The SCMP's analytical question ('Did US pressure at the summit force the KMT's hand on the budget?') represents a competing interpretation of the May 9 budget vote: KMT may have passed the budget partly to avoid being seen as a liability before the summit rather than from sovereign defense conviction. This framing would weaken the DPP's national security messaging narrative if it becomes dominant.",
